=== entry 651cc2734134
kind: policy
rules: delegate, background, store, pr-wait, loop
intent: Translation: workers are async delegate_task children whose taskId and childThreadId the coordinator keeps; the Project tracker and store become a `notes.md` tracker in the shared per-repo pstack state folder; PR and CI event subscriptions are watch_pull_request with a heartbeat, since a merge ends the watch without a wake. Policy (reload-the-orchestrator-after): reload the orchestrator after auto-compaction. `.claude/orchestrator-state.md` is the one file that survives compaction; the coordinator rewrites it whenever the program's state changes, and the compact hook in references/compact-reload.md prints it back. Preserve calling no scripts, no polling loops, and never relaying between agents.
--- upstream
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `Task`. Keep the program's state in the Project tracker (`notes.md`) and the Project store. Watch each PR with PR and CI event subscriptions when your harness has them. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.
--- patched
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `delegate_task`, `mode: "async"`, and the whole kickoff in `task`, and keep each returned `taskId` and `childThreadId`. Keep the program's state in a tracker, `notes.md`, in the store (`~/.local/state/pstack/<repo>/orchestrate/<project-slug>/`, where `<repo>` is `basename "$(dirname "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)")"`, created if missing). Watch each PR with `watch_pull_request` and end the turn, with a `schedule_task` heartbeat of 20 to 30 minutes, because a merge ends the watch without a wake. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.

`$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-state.md` is the one file that survives compaction, so it lives in the project and not the store. Rewrite it whenever the program's state changes, under 6,000 characters. It holds the goal, the path to `notes.md`, the live agents with their `taskId`s, the Pods, the open PRs, the decisions waiting on the user, and the next steps. The compact hook in `references/compact-reload.md` prints it back; install that hook in the project's `.claude/settings.json`, or the file is never read.

=== entry ca155dd357b3
kind: translation
rules: isolation, verify-surface, team-kit, transcripts, cursor-only, model-effort, same-model-review
intent: A worker that writes gets its own worktree the way upstream defaulted workers to a cloud VM, and the list of reasons to stay on this machine becomes a note that every child runs here; runtime checks drive the project's verification skill, preview tools, or the shell. The effort pick becomes the role line's ceiling. Policy: the verifier runs on its worker's model and effort, not a different family (rule same-model-review). Keep one owner per PR, the fresh-worker kickoff, the three-level cap and rolling window, fork-or-message, and start-with-one-unit.
--- upstream
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Run workers on a new cloud VM (`environment: "cloud"`) unless the work needs this machine: a `control-ui` or `control-cli` runtime check (from `cursor-team-kit`), local transcripts, a simulator, or auth that exists only here. Pick each subagent's reasoning level and fast or standard speed for the job, per the Subagents section.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Fork when new work needs a worker's built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process. Message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on a different model family. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.
--- patched
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. A worker that writes gets its own worktree (`isolation: "worktree"`), a checkout of your current local commit, so commit first or have the kickoff name a pushed branch to check out. A worker that only reads gets no `isolation` and shares the checkout. Every child runs on this machine, so a runtime check (the project's own verification skill, T3's `preview_*` tools, or the shell for a CLI), reading transcripts with `t3_thread_read`, a simulator, and auth that exists only here work from any worker. Set each subagent's effort for the job per the Subagents section, lowering its line's effort for routine work and never raising it.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Fork when new work needs a worker's built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process. Message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on the same model and effort as the worker it checks, unless the user named another. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.
